--- Repro (Admin POS, worktree backend :8721, DB multi_food_db_qa_nears3224) ---
POST /admin/pos/empty-cart -> 200 []
POST /admin/pos/add-to-cart {id:111, quantity:0} -> HTTP 500 {"message":"Something went wrong"}
  (item WAS still written into session cart with quantity=0 despite the 500)
POST /admin/pos/add-delivery-address {...} -> 200
POST /admin/pos/order?store_id=2 {user_id:409, type:cash} -> HTTP 403 {"errors":[{"code":"quantity","message":"Invalid item quantity"}]}
  (place_order's OWN call site correctly checks status_code===403 before indexing, so the
   final order-placement rejection is clean and correct — AC7's literal ask passes here)

Root cause: Admin/app/Services/Order/OrderTaxService.php::setPosCalculatedTax() (line ~327-328)
calls $this->detailBuilder->build($carts, new PosOrderContext(...)) and immediately indexes
$order_details['total_addon_price'] with NO data_get($order_details,'status_code')===403 guard —
unlike its two siblings in the same file, getCalculatedTax() (line 234) and
setOrderEditCalculatedTax() (line 382), which both correctly check status_code===403 before
indexing. When the cart holds a non-positive/non-numeric-quantity line (reachable only via a
crafted request to add-to-cart/update-quantity, since the real POS UI's quantity inputs are
client-min-clamped to 1), build() returns the short error array
{status_code:403, code:'quantity', message:...} instead of the full order_details shape, and
$order_details['total_addon_price'] throws "Undefined array key" -> Laravel converts the PHP
warning to an ErrorException -> HTTP 500 "Something went wrong".

Blast radius: setPosCalculatedTax() is called from POSController::addToCart(),
::updateQuantity() and ::removeFromCart() in BOTH Admin/app/Http/Controllers/Admin/POSController.php
and Admin/app/Http/Controllers/Vendor/POSController.php (same injected OrderTaxService, same
method, same missing guard). LIVE-reproduced on BOTH: Admin POS add-to-cart{id:111,quantity:0} ->
500 above; Vendor Store Panel (vendor-panel/pos) add-to-cart{id:111,quantity:-1} -> same 500
"Something went wrong" (Vendor's own place_order?quantity=-1 submit still correctly 403'd
afterward: {"errors":[{"code":"quantity","message":"Invalid item quantity"}]}). Practical effect:
once a bad-quantity line lands in a POS cart via a crafted request, every subsequent normal POS
cart action that recalculates tax (add another item, change quantity, remove an item) 500s until
the operator hits "Clear Cart" (emptyCart() does not call setPosCalculatedTax) — a stuck-cart
defect on top of the 500 itself.

CORRECTED classification (initial read below was wrong — keeping the correction visible rather
than silently rewriting history): Pre-existing before this ticket? YES. Checked
Admin/app/Traits/PlaceNewOrder.php @ c68e4d491 (pre-refactor): setPosCalculatedTax() there
(line 2104-2128) has the BYTE-IDENTICAL missing status_code===403 guard before indexing
$order_details['total_addon_price'] — this ticket's OrderTaxService::setPosCalculatedTax() is a
structural port of that exact method, not new code. AND makePosOrderDetails() (the pre-refactor
callee) already returned the SAME short error-array shape (no 'total_addon_price' key) via
pre-existing 'different_stores', 'prescription', and a PRE-EXISTING max-quantity 'quantity' guard
(different message: "maximum_cart_quantity_limit_over", unrelated to this ticket's non-positive
guard) — so this exact 500 was already reachable pre-ticket (e.g. cross-store cart tamper, or
tampering an item past its maximum_cart_quantity). This ticket's new non-positive-quantity guard
is simply ONE MORE way to trigger an already-broken caller-side check, not a new introduction.
Filed as regression_bug (pre-existing, unrelated to this ticket's own correctness) — does not
affect this run's verdict per the QA-agent lane rules, but flagged here with full repro + root
cause since it is a real, reproducible defect (and touches the same AUD-SEC-36 quantity-guard
surface this ticket partially closes, so worth prioritizing).

[Superseded — kept for audit trail, DO NOT rely on this paragraph, see CORRECTED above]
"Pre-existing before this ticket? NO — pre-refactor, makePosOrderDetails() (PlaceNewOrder trait)
had no line-quantity guard at all for POS, so this code path could never previously return a 403
error array; setPosCalculatedTax() only started receiving that shape once THIS ticket's AC6b
uniform-guard landed, so the missing status_code check is a genuine gap in this ticket's own new
code, not an inherited pre-existing defect." — this was written before checking whether
makePosOrderDetails had OTHER pre-existing error-return paths (different_stores/prescription/
max-quantity); it did, so the conclusion above was wrong and is corrected.
